WEBVTT

00:00:16.642 --> 00:00:17.902
Hi. I'm Kevin.

00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:20.502
I'll be walking you through how every department

00:00:20.522 --> 00:00:21.602
in this building connects.

00:00:22.402 --> 00:00:23.702
I've been asked to act natural.

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:25.502
This is me acting natural.

00:00:26.762 --> 00:00:28.122
He's doing great.

00:00:28.822 --> 00:00:29.952
Relax, Kevin.

00:00:29.972 --> 00:00:31.202
I'm the narrator.

00:00:31.252 --> 00:00:34.048
And look at that: you're parked, forks all the

00:00:34.068 --> 00:00:36.011
way down and tilted forward.

00:00:36.031 --> 00:00:37.263
Textbook.

00:00:37.564 --> 00:00:40.787
Plant rule. When parked, booms and forks are all

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.749
the way down and tilted forward.

00:00:42.769 --> 00:00:45.096
When in motion, they're at least eighteen inches

00:00:45.116 --> 00:00:47.952
off the ground and tilted all the way back.

00:00:48.602 --> 00:00:50.238
I'll take it from here.

00:00:50.964 --> 00:00:53.248
This is a coil of hot-rolled steel rod.

00:00:53.268 --> 00:00:55.125
Right now, it is not a bolt.

00:00:55.145 --> 00:00:56.320
It is not a fastener.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.161
It is not anything yet.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:00:59.984
By the time it leaves this building,

00:01:00.004 --> 00:01:02.374
it'll be ready to become parts that hold cars,

00:01:02.394 --> 00:01:04.208
bridges, and equipment together.

00:01:04.228 --> 00:01:05.650
No pressure.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.618
Some pressure.

00:01:08.644 --> 00:01:09.974
Episode one.

00:01:09.994 --> 00:01:13.362
Please hold your applause until the end.

00:01:14.501 --> 00:01:17.766
Our plant receives hot-rolled steel rod coils and

00:01:17.786 --> 00:01:20.454
turns them into rod or wire for cold heading into

00:01:20.474 --> 00:01:22.166
fasteners and other components.

00:01:22.186 --> 00:01:24.512
Every department's output becomes the next department's

00:01:24.532 --> 00:01:26.987
input, and the condition of the material at every

00:01:27.007 --> 00:01:29.163
hand-off decides whether the customer can form

00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:30.583
the final coil.

00:01:30.683 --> 00:01:32.441
The path isn't always the same.

00:01:32.461 --> 00:01:35.257
Some coils are annealed first, some are drawn and

00:01:35.277 --> 00:01:38.457
come back for another anneal, and some are coat-only.

00:01:38.477 --> 00:01:41.316
It depends on the grade, the customer's specification,

00:01:41.336 --> 00:01:43.601
and the final properties they need.

00:01:43.926 --> 00:01:46.039
Which means every department in this building has

00:01:46.059 --> 00:01:48.685
a customer. One rule covers all of them,

00:01:48.705 --> 00:01:50.349
and it has three parts.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:53.510
Never knowingly pass a defect downstream.

00:01:53.658 --> 00:01:57.393
Treat every internal hand-off as a customer delivery.

00:01:57.413 --> 00:01:59.782
And never accept your current level of knowledge

00:01:59.802 --> 00:02:01.474
as the ceiling.

00:02:01.795 --> 00:02:04.993
That's technically three rules.

00:02:05.186 --> 00:02:08.147
We are counting it as one.

00:02:08.698 --> 00:02:11.238
Every coil arrives identified by grade,

00:02:11.258 --> 00:02:12.987
heat number, and size.

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:16.272
Job one: verify that identity, check for obvious

00:02:16.292 --> 00:02:19.569
transit damage, and store the coil so it's protected.

00:02:19.791 --> 00:02:22.245
Because a misidentified or damaged coil doesn't

00:02:22.265 --> 00:02:25.274
stay here. It rides along through every department,

00:02:25.294 --> 00:02:26.725
and out to the customer.

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:28.330
Get it right at the start.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:29.637
Delivered.

00:02:30.322 --> 00:02:33.758
Annealing softens the steel so it can be cold-formed.

00:02:33.778 --> 00:02:35.977
Think of it as a spa day for steel.

00:02:35.997 --> 00:02:39.217
Pusher furnaces run continuously, for steady production.

00:02:39.237 --> 00:02:41.777
Batch furnaces run a full charge through a complete

00:02:41.797 --> 00:02:43.901
cycle, for flexibility.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.684
Spheroidize annealing changes the carbide structure

00:02:46.704 --> 00:02:48.945
so the steel can flow in a heading die instead

00:02:48.965 --> 00:02:49.950
of cracking.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.168
There's also stress-relief annealing.

00:02:52.188 --> 00:02:54.302
Protective atmospheres keep the surface from losing

00:02:54.322 --> 00:02:56.862
carbon, which is called decarburization,

00:02:56.882 --> 00:02:58.569
and from building heavy scale.

00:02:58.589 --> 00:03:00.707
The steel gets its own atmosphere.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.795
Some of us don't even get our own parking spot.

00:03:04.045 --> 00:03:05.400
I don't.

00:03:05.638 --> 00:03:07.666
Annealing is a customer too.

00:03:07.686 --> 00:03:10.183
Some drawn material comes back here for an intermediate

00:03:10.203 --> 00:03:11.634
or final anneal.

00:03:11.654 --> 00:03:12.796
Delivered.

00:03:13.496 --> 00:03:15.695
Hot-rolled rod carries mill scale,

00:03:15.715 --> 00:03:18.297
iron oxides that formed when the rod cooled at

00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:20.601
the mill. The iron did it to itself.

00:03:20.621 --> 00:03:22.008
Iron-ic.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.608
Coils go through sulfuric acid tanks to remove

00:03:24.628 --> 00:03:26.229
it, then rinses.

00:03:26.249 --> 00:03:28.875
Scale left behind keeps coatings from sticking,

00:03:28.895 --> 00:03:31.648
and in drawing, it scores the dies.

00:03:31.668 --> 00:03:33.588
Rinsing matters just as much.

00:03:33.608 --> 00:03:36.276
Leftover acid can cause corrosion or staining,

00:03:36.296 --> 00:03:39.050
hurt coating adhesion, and create defects later

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:41.226
in drawing or at the customer.

00:03:41.246 --> 00:03:43.188
Coils should leave the line neutralized,

00:03:43.208 --> 00:03:45.686
and dry enough for the next step.

00:03:46.413 --> 00:03:49.081
After cleaning comes coating: pickle-and-lime,

00:03:49.101 --> 00:03:52.409
phosphate-and-polymer, or phosphate-and-lube.

00:03:52.429 --> 00:03:54.499
The coating lets the steel pass through the drawing

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:57.102
dies without direct metal-to-metal contact,

00:03:57.122 --> 00:03:58.722
which causes galling.

00:03:58.824 --> 00:04:01.534
The steel and the die are on a strict no-touching

00:04:01.554 --> 00:04:02.430
policy.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.396
Coverage has to be even, and it has to stick.

00:04:05.416 --> 00:04:08.412
The draw room is the coating line's direct customer.

00:04:08.432 --> 00:04:11.100
A sub-par lime coating doesn't just look bad.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.063
It spikes the friction in the die box,

00:04:13.083 --> 00:04:15.196
and can cause a break on the draw block.

00:04:15.216 --> 00:04:16.794
On somebody else's shift.

00:04:16.814 --> 00:04:19.294
And we'll know if it was you.

00:04:19.855 --> 00:04:21.713
Protect the next department in line,

00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:23.803
and you protect the whole company.

00:04:23.823 --> 00:04:24.826
Delivered.

00:04:24.846 --> 00:04:27.717
Now. Let's drop in on the draw room.

00:04:30.854 --> 00:04:31.775
Oh.

00:04:31.975 --> 00:04:33.384
Didn't see ya there.

00:04:33.404 --> 00:04:34.493
Sorry, Kevin.

00:04:34.513 --> 00:04:37.272
Never sneak up on somebody running a machine.

00:04:37.292 --> 00:04:39.848
Fine. I'll take the draw room.

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:43.202
In the draw department, we reduce the diameter

00:04:43.222 --> 00:04:45.634
of the steel by pulling it through dies.

00:04:45.654 --> 00:04:47.852
The volume stays essentially the same,

00:04:47.872 --> 00:04:49.730
so when the cross-section gets smaller,

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.425
the coil gets longer.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.372
Our tolerance is typically

00:04:54.311 --> 00:04:56.897
plus or minus two thousandths of an inch.

00:04:56.917 --> 00:04:59.159
A sheet of office paper is about four thousandths

00:04:59.179 --> 00:05:00.187
thick.

00:05:00.374 --> 00:05:03.077
So the allowed tolerance is about half the thickness

00:05:03.097 --> 00:05:05.540
of a sheet of paper, or less.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:12.191
Holding that takes stable dies, consistent lubrication

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:14.367
from the coating, proper alignment,

00:05:14.387 --> 00:05:15.903
and controlled reduction.

00:05:15.923 --> 00:05:18.762
Upstream problems, like residual scale,

00:05:18.782 --> 00:05:22.317
poor coating, or handling gouges, show up fast.

00:05:22.563 --> 00:05:24.932
Drawing also work-hardens the steel.

00:05:24.952 --> 00:05:27.620
It gets stronger, but less ductile.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.137
That's why some material goes back for an intermediate

00:05:30.157 --> 00:05:31.954
or final anneal.

00:05:32.488 --> 00:05:34.729
And no matter how long you've run a block,

00:05:34.749 --> 00:05:37.161
don't assume you know exactly how a particular

00:05:37.181 --> 00:05:39.678
heat of alloy will react to a heavy draft.

00:05:39.698 --> 00:05:42.153
Understand the why behind your numbers.

00:05:42.173 --> 00:05:44.657
Otherwise, the steel will humble you.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.193
It has humbled me.

00:05:46.213 --> 00:05:47.708
Twice this week.

00:05:47.728 --> 00:05:49.438
Narrator. You're up.

00:05:49.458 --> 00:05:50.376
Thanks, Kevin.

00:05:50.396 --> 00:05:52.240
You're a real draw.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:53.465
Don't.

00:05:54.128 --> 00:05:56.838
Coils move between departments by overhead crane

00:05:56.858 --> 00:05:58.161
and high-low.

00:05:58.181 --> 00:06:00.977
Dragging a coil across concrete, spearing it with

00:06:00.997 --> 00:06:04.177
forks or a boom, or dropping it creates dents,

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:07.161
gouges, flat spots, and distorted wraps.

00:06:07.181 --> 00:06:09.209
That damage is usually permanent.

00:06:09.229 --> 00:06:11.812
It causes trouble in dies, in furnaces,

00:06:11.832 --> 00:06:15.140
in coating, and when the customer unwinds the coil.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:16.889
Coils don't hold grudges.

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:17.999
They hold dents.

00:06:18.019 --> 00:06:19.298
Forever.

00:06:20.028 --> 00:06:22.141
The lab takes two kinds of samples,

00:06:22.161 --> 00:06:23.506
at two different moments.

00:06:23.526 --> 00:06:25.853
Green samples are taken before we make any physical

00:06:25.873 --> 00:06:27.261
change to the steel.

00:06:27.281 --> 00:06:29.778
They verify the integrity of the incoming material,

00:06:29.798 --> 00:06:32.072
for example by looking for decarb.

00:06:32.099 --> 00:06:33.359
Which is not a diet.

00:06:33.379 --> 00:06:36.993
Although, technically, it is the steel losing carbon.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:39.582
Tensile samples are taken from material going out

00:06:39.602 --> 00:06:42.697
the door, so we know, and record, the strength

00:06:42.717 --> 00:06:44.561
and ductility of what we ship.

00:06:44.581 --> 00:06:46.993
Either way, samples have to be accurate,

00:06:47.013 --> 00:06:49.639
representative, and undamaged, or they can lead

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:52.241
to the wrong accept or reject decision.

00:06:52.261 --> 00:06:54.503
Floor checks for surface, diameter,

00:06:54.523 --> 00:06:56.935
coating, and identification happen all through

00:06:56.955 --> 00:06:58.449
the process, too.

00:06:58.469 --> 00:07:01.009
Our plant is held to our production certification

00:07:01.029 --> 00:07:04.721
standards. So quality isn't just the lab's job.

00:07:04.741 --> 00:07:05.988
It's everyone's.

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:08.719
Whether it's wire for critical automotive applications,

00:07:08.739 --> 00:07:11.876
or rod for non-critical U-bolts and pins,

00:07:11.896 --> 00:07:15.613
a defect ignored early compounds down the line.

00:07:16.333 --> 00:07:18.617
Cold heading magnifies imperfections.

00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:20.579
Watch for residual mill scale.

00:07:20.599 --> 00:07:21.689
Coating defects.

00:07:21.709 --> 00:07:22.926
Handling damage.

00:07:22.946 --> 00:07:24.921
Die lines and scoring.

00:07:24.947 --> 00:07:27.913
Corrosion or staining from incomplete rinsing.

00:07:27.933 --> 00:07:30.431
And tangles or distorted wraps.

00:07:30.451 --> 00:07:32.522
Any of these can become a crack starter in a heading

00:07:32.542 --> 00:07:35.447
die. Every department shares the job of preventing

00:07:35.467 --> 00:07:37.793
them, or catching them.

00:07:38.523 --> 00:07:40.807
Finished coils are banded, protected,

00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:43.025
labeled, and prepared for shipment.

00:07:43.045 --> 00:07:45.244
Packaging protects the material in transit,

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:47.120
and it presents the product professionally.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.680
A customer who has invested several thousand dollars

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:53.294
in a coil does not want it to look poorly handled.

00:07:53.314 --> 00:07:55.342
Some of our customers run just-in-time,

00:07:55.362 --> 00:07:57.160
with less inventory on hand.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:59.463
So a coil scrapped at the very end doesn't just

00:07:59.483 --> 00:08:00.658
waste steel.

00:08:00.678 --> 00:08:02.536
It can disrupt a supply chain that was counting

00:08:02.556 --> 00:08:03.496
on it.

00:08:03.516 --> 00:08:04.477
Delivered.

00:08:04.497 --> 00:08:06.169
For real this time.

00:08:06.904 --> 00:08:09.188
What we make becomes fasteners and components in

00:08:09.208 --> 00:08:12.814
automobiles, structures, bridges, and equipment.

00:08:12.834 --> 00:08:15.918
A failure in service can have serious safety consequences.

00:08:15.938 --> 00:08:18.435
And a quality problem that shuts down a Big Three

00:08:18.455 --> 00:08:21.763
assembly plant can cost tens of thousands of dollars

00:08:21.783 --> 00:08:23.112
a minute.

00:08:23.885 --> 00:08:26.383
But none of the tonnage or throughput numbers matter

00:08:26.403 --> 00:08:28.943
if safety or integrity is compromised.

00:08:28.963 --> 00:08:31.844
We never knowingly ship a defective product.

00:08:31.864 --> 00:08:34.447
We put the health, safety, and environment of our

00:08:34.467 --> 00:08:36.740
coworkers and our community first.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.769
So if something isn't right, you have the authority,

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:42.244
and the obligation, to stop the machine.

00:08:42.264 --> 00:08:44.108
The company backs you.

00:08:44.857 --> 00:08:46.202
One more thing, folks.

00:08:46.222 --> 00:08:48.567
We're all steel in this together.

00:08:48.952 --> 00:08:50.964
That one was bad.

00:08:51.208 --> 00:08:53.833
Never knowingly pass a defect downstream.

00:08:53.853 --> 00:08:56.734
Treat every hand-off like a customer delivery.

00:08:56.754 --> 00:08:59.045
And never assume you're done learning.

00:08:59.588 --> 00:09:00.634
Remember.

00:09:00.654 --> 00:09:03.792
The difference between ordinary and extraordinary

00:09:03.812 --> 00:09:05.626
is that little bit extra.

00:09:05.646 --> 00:09:06.608
That's all.

00:09:06.628 --> 00:09:07.716
Delivered.
